{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Retraction of the previous comment, and the actual root cause\n\nThe comment I posted immediately before this one is **wrong on its central claim** and should not be acted on. Correcting it, and recording what the evidence actually shows.\n\n### What I got wrong\n\nI claimed the guard is blind for the current day because it evaluates thresholds against a Cost Explorer figure that CE does not settle until the day has passed. That reasoning was built on the name of the `ce_spend` field in the state parameter without reading the code that consumes it.\n\nThe deployed handler computes `spend = max(ce_spend, metered)`. Its own module docstring states the intent plainly: CE exists to catch anything the token metering misses. CE is deliberately the slow secondary signal, and it is refreshed only when a minimum interval has elapsed or when the metered figure is already close to the hard cap — it is not polled on every tick, by design. The fast, governing signal is `metered`, priced from CloudWatch per-`ModelId` token counts.\n\nI also reported the Lambda as polling hourly. It runs on a **five-minute** cadence. The hourly reading was an artifact of which log lines I happened to sample.\n\nBoth of my supporting observations were real but did not mean what I said. `ce_spend` was flat because CE is intentionally refreshed rarely. The metered figure was flat because Bedrock traffic had genuinely stopped: `AWS/Bedrock` `Invocations` in five-minute buckets shows activity in the two buckets ending 15:45Z and **zero invocations in every bucket after**, which matches the exact minute the metered figure stopped advancing. The guard was tracking reality correctly the entire time.\n\n### The real defect, confirmed by measurement\n\nThis ticket's premise — that the rate card carries no Anthropic entry — is **true but not sufficient**, because it omits `SEED_RATES`. Rate resolution has four ordered steps: an observed rate for the exact model and token direction, then the model's published seed rate, then the model's observed input rate scaled by a direction multiplier, then the fail-closed `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K`. The Lambda's `SEED_RATES` environment variable carries Anthropic needles, so most Claude traffic resolves at step 2 and never reaches the fallback. The ticket's conclusion that *every* invocation logs a fail-closed fallback does not hold.\n\nWhat does hold is narrower and sharper. The configured seed needles are `claude-haiku-4-5`, `claude-opus`, and `claude-sonnet-4-6`. The account emits metrics for a Sonnet model at version **4-5**, and the needle `claude-sonnet-4-6` does not match it. Matching is substring-based, so a needle version-pinned one minor version forward silently fails to cover the model actually in use. The `claude-opus` needle has no such problem precisely because it is not version-pinned.\n\nFiltering the Lambda's own logs over 24 hours for the fail-closed message returns **292 events**, and they resolve to exactly **one** model across all four token directions:\n\n- `us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0` — `InputTokenCount`, `OutputTokenCount`, `CacheReadInputTokenCount`, `CacheWriteInputTokenCount`\n\nNo other model reaches the fallback.\n\n### Why this matters, and why it is currently latent\n\n`UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K` is set deliberately high so unpriced models fail closed. That is correct behaviour for a genuinely unknown model, but this model is not unknown — it is a mainstream model whose seed needle is merely mis-versioned. So whenever that model carries real traffic, the metered figure overstates spend, and the guard trips earlier than actual usage warrants. That is exactly the \"pauses work it did not need to pause\" outcome this ticket describes, but the cause is a version-pinned needle, not a missing rate card.\n\nIt is latent right now: that model emitted no tokens today, so today's metered figure is not inflated by it. The fallback log events come from the earlier part of the 24-hour window. This will produce a wrong result the next time that model is used, not today.\n\n### Suggested fix direction\n\nMake the Sonnet seed needle version-agnostic in the same way the Opus needle already is, so it covers the model family rather than one minor version. A regression test should assert that every `ModelId` the account currently emits metrics for resolves at a step **above** the fail-closed fallback, so a future model rotation fails the test rather than silently repricing traffic. Treating the fallback as an error signal rather than a warning would also surface this immediately instead of 292 log lines into it.\n\nSequencing note unchanged: `infra/terraform/bedrock-spend-cap/handler.py` is under active modification in Gerrit change 2984, which is adding this module's first test coverage. This fix belongs after that lands so the two do not conflict.\n\nNo thresholds, rates, or figures are recorded here; the repository and tickets branch are public."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789754016323784795,"uuid":"4d0bb4e0-9d57-4b42-a0a1-513c928bcf58"}